# Commit 13: Generate real field-to-field mapping expressions in Message Mappings

**Branch**: `Work-on-Parameters-and-Remaining-Components`
**Scope**: `packages/compiler` (the `@cpi-ai/compiler` package)
**Builds on**: commit 12 (`fix(compiler): stop inventing fake XSD references
in auto-generated Message Mappings`), which fixed the `.mmap`'s schema
LINKS. This commit fixes the missing field-level mapping EXPRESSIONS.

---

## 1. What broke (reported live)

Example prompt: *"When a customer is created or updated in the source
system, validate the customer information, enrich it with additional
details, transform it into the format required by the target system, and
send the updated customer record to the target system."*

Generated flow: `Customer(Name, Email)` -> Message Mapping ->
`Customer(Name, Email)` (identical structure). After commit 12, the
Message Mapping editor opened successfully with correct Source/Target
structures. But the target fields `Name` and `Email` showed in **RED**
with no mapping expressions -- the mapping component/resource existed, but
nothing was actually mapped.

## 2. Root cause

`MappingResource.generateProperSapFormat()` (see commit 12) links the
source/target SCHEMAS by emitting exactly one `<brick>` in
`<transformation>`, connecting the target ROOT element to the source ROOT
element (e.g. `/Target` <- `/Source`). That establishes the structural
correspondence SAP needs to open the editor, but it is not a field mapping
-- it never touches child elements like `Name`/`Email`. SAP has no way to
know those fields correspond, so it renders them unmapped.

Evidence for what a REAL field mapping brick looks like, from the supplied
reference export (`Send Inbound Daily Sales from Russia 3PL to S4HANA and
DataSphere (1).zip` -> `src/main/resources/mapping/MM_Mat_3PL_to_S4HANA.mmap`):
```xml
<brick gid="0" path="/MBGMCR04/IDOC/E1MBGMCR/E1BP2017_GM_ITEM_CREATE/ENTRY_QNT" type="Dst">
  <viewData x="200" y="40"/>
  <arg>
    <brick gid="0" path="/Records/Record/Sales_QTY" type="Src"><viewData x="50" y="40"/></brick>
  </arg>
  <group/>
</brick>
```
A direct field-to-field mapping is one `<brick type="Dst">` per TARGET leaf
path, whose `<arg>` is a `<brick type="Src">` for the corresponding SOURCE
leaf path -- structurally identical to the existing root-level brick, just
at a deeper path. (The same reference file also has function-wrapped
bricks -- `fname="TransformDate"`, `fname="getProperty"`, etc. -- for
computed values; those are out of scope here, see Known Limitations.)

---

## 3. The fix

### `MappingResource.ts`

- New `MappingFieldRule` interface: `{ sourcePath: string; targetPath: string }`,
  each a path relative to that side's root element (e.g. `"Name"`, or
  nested `"Address/City"`).
- Constructor gained an optional `fieldMappings?: MappingFieldRule[]`
  parameter. Throws immediately if `fieldMappings` is non-empty but either
  `sourceSchema` or `targetSchema` is missing -- a field path is only
  meaningful once the real root element name is known.
- `generateProperSapFormat()` now emits one additional direct-mapping
  `<brick>` per `fieldMappings` entry (in the same shape as the reference
  evidence above), alongside the existing root-level structural brick.
  With no `fieldMappings`, behavior is byte-for-byte identical to commit 12
  (no new bricks) -- fully backward compatible.
- Field names are **never inferred** by comparing source/target structures
  for look-alike names. Every mapping in the output corresponds to an
  entry the caller explicitly listed -- an auto-guessed match that happened
  to be wrong would itself be a fake mapping.

### `ComponentFactory.ts`

- `ResourceConfig` gained `fieldMappings?: Array<{ sourcePath, targetPath }>`
  (meaningful only for `type: 'mapping'` entries).
- New `xsdDeclaresElement(xsdContent, elementName)` helper: a lightweight
  text check (not a full XSD parse) confirming an `<xs:element name="...">`
  with that name exists somewhere in the given schema content.
- New `resolveMappingFieldRules()`, called when building a `mapping`
  resource in `fromJson()`:
  - No `fieldMappings` given -> `undefined` (falls back to schema-linking
    only, exactly as before this commit).
  - `fieldMappings` given without both `sourceSchema`/`targetSchema` ->
    **throws** with a clear explanation.
  - Any rule missing `sourcePath` or `targetPath` -> **throws**.
  - Any path segment (on either side) that isn't declared as a real
    `<xs:element>` in that side's actual XSD content -> **throws**, naming
    the missing field and which XSD it should be in. A Message Mapping can
    no longer claim to map a field that doesn't exist in the real schema.

### `capabilities.ts`

- `MessageMapping`'s `notes` extended to document `fieldMappings`: exact
  shape, that it requires the schema links from commit 12, that each
  segment must be a real element in the XSD, and that it is never
  auto-inferred.

---

## 4. Files changed (this package only)

**Modified**:
- `src/model/MappingResource.ts` -- `MappingFieldRule`, constructor param +
  validation, `generateProperSapFormat()` emits per-field bricks.
- `src/factory/ComponentFactory.ts` -- `ResourceConfig.fieldMappings`,
  `xsdDeclaresElement()`, `resolveMappingFieldRules()`, wired into the
  `'mapping'` resource case in `fromJson()`.
- `src/api/capabilities.ts` -- `MessageMapping` component `notes` extended.
- `src/index.ts` -- exports the new `MappingFieldRule` type.
- `test-messagemapping-regression.ts` -- 6 new scenarios ([7]-[11], plus
  updated header comments); all 5 pre-existing scenarios re-verified
  unchanged.

**Deliberately not touched**: `MessageMapping.ts` (the flow-step
`callActivity` component -- confirmed it only sets BPMN properties like
`mappingname`/`mappingpath`/`mappinguri` and never touches `.mmap`
content, so it needed no change), everything already listed as untouched
in commit 12 (BPMN mapper/writer layers, XSD/XmlValidator, every adapter,
sequence-flow fix, Fiori UI, CAP controller).

---

## 5. Testing

`test-messagemapping-regression.ts`, scenarios [7]-[11] (in addition to
the 5 pre-existing ones, all still passing):

7. Direct field mappings `Customer/Name -> Target/FullName` and
   `Customer/Email -> Target/ContactEmail` produce real
   `<brick path="/Target/FullName" type="Dst"><arg><brick path="/Customer/Name" type="Src">...`
   expressions in the generated `.mmap` (checked against the literal
   generated XML, not just "does it compile").
8. Nested field mapping: `Order/ShipTo/City -> Invoice/Delivery/City`
   produces a correctly-pathed nested brick.
9. `fieldMappings` referencing a field name that doesn't exist in the XSD
   (`DoesNotExistField`) is rejected with a clear error.
10. `fieldMappings` given without `sourceXsd`/`targetXsd` schema references
    is rejected.
11. A full, real `.mmap` with function-based bindings
    (`fname="TransformDate"`, etc. -- exactly the pattern from the
    reference export) continues to pass through **completely unchanged**;
    `fieldMappings` has zero effect on real, already-complete mapping
    content.

Also manually reproduced the exact reported bug shape end-to-end
(`Customer(Name, Email) -> Customer(Name, Email)`, identical structure,
with `fieldMappings` for both fields) and inspected the raw generated
`<transformation>` section directly:
```xml
<transformation>
  <brick gid="0" path="/Customer" type="Dst"><arg><brick gid="0" path="/Customer" type="Src"/></arg><group/></brick>
  <brick gid="0" path="/Customer/Name" type="Dst"><arg><brick gid="0" path="/Customer/Name" type="Src"/></arg><group/></brick>
  <brick gid="0" path="/Customer/Email" type="Dst"><arg><brick gid="0" path="/Customer/Email" type="Src"/></arg><group/></brick>
</transformation>
```
Both target fields now have a real, direct mapping expression instead of
being left unmapped.

**Full pre-existing regression suite re-run after this change -- zero
regressions**: `test-amqp-regression.ts`, `test-jms-regression.ts`,
`test-sequence-flow-regression.ts`, `test-rfc-regression.ts`,
`test-jdbc-regression.ts`, `test-jdbc-ai-scenarios-regression.ts`,
`test-naming-version-regression.ts`, `test-customer-flow-regression.ts`,
`test-processdirect-regression.ts`,
`test-localintegrationprocess-regression.ts`, `test/run-rt-003-tests.ts`
(9/9), `test/run-mapping-regression-simple.ts` (3/3). `npx tsc --noEmit`
and `npm run build` both clean throughout.

---

## 6. Example corrected JSON

```json
{
  "resources": [
    { "type": "xsd", "name": "Customer.xsd", "content": "..." },
    { "type": "xsd", "name": "Target.xsd", "content": "..." },
    {
      "type": "mapping",
      "name": "Customer_to_Target.mmap",
      "content": "<mapping></mapping>",
      "sourceXsd": "Customer.xsd",
      "sourceRootElement": "Customer",
      "targetXsd": "Target.xsd",
      "targetRootElement": "Target",
      "fieldMappings": [
        { "sourcePath": "Name", "targetPath": "Name" },
        { "sourcePath": "Email", "targetPath": "Email" }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

---

## 7. Known limitations

- Only **direct** field-to-field mappings are generated (target field =
  source field, verbatim). Function-based mappings (date formatting,
  constants, `getProperty`, concatenation, etc. -- all present in the
  reference export) are NOT auto-generated by this fix; they were never in
  scope for the reported defect (which was about missing mappings for a
  structurally identical Customer/Customer case). Real `.mmap` content
  that already contains such functions continues to pass through
  completely unchanged (scenario 11), so nothing already working is
  broken.
- `xsdDeclaresElement()` is a text-level check, not a full XSD parse: it
  confirms the element name exists somewhere in the schema, not that a
  multi-segment path (e.g. `"Address/City"`) sits at that exact nested
  position. This is an intentional, documented trade-off (this compiler
  carries no XML Schema parser) that still catches the concrete failure
  mode of mapping a field that doesn't exist in the schema at all.
- `fieldMappings` is caller-supplied and explicit by design -- this
  compiler does not attempt to auto-detect "identical structure" and map
  fields by matching names, since a wrong automatic match (e.g. two
  same-named fields that aren't actually semantically equivalent) would
  itself be a fake mapping. The AI/system generating the flow JSON from a
  natural-language prompt is responsible for stating each field
  correspondence explicitly.
- Not re-verified against a live SAP Integration Suite import in this
  session (no tenant access) -- verified structurally against the
  generated `.mmap`'s raw XML instead, matching this project's established
  standard, and directly against the real reference export's own brick
  format for a direct mapping.
